; TYPED SYMBOLIC CODE
; language specification, working draft
;
; Sections 0 to 2 say how this file and the language are written down.
; Sections 3 to 9 specify the language. Section 10 holds complete example
; programs, and section 11 lists everything that is still undecided.

;;;; 0 how to read this file

;;; 0.1 headers and numbering
; 1. A line starting with four semicolons starts a section, three start an
;    entry, and two start a sub-entry. In VS Code, each header folds up to
;    the next header of the same or a higher level.
; 2. Rules are numbered inside the innermost header that holds them: rule 3
;    of entry 5.5 is 5.5.3, and rule 1 of sub-entry 4.4.2 is 4.4.2.1.
; 3. An entry that has sub-entries keeps all of its rules in them.
; 4. A number in parentheses, such as (2.4.2), points at an entry or a rule.
; 5. (todo) after a header marks an entry that is not designed yet.

;;; 0.2 grammar notation
; General forms mix real syntax with placeholders.
; 1. <Name> is a syntactic category (2.5). Some categories take a type:
;    <Value T> is a value of type T. A category written without a type, such
;    as <Value>, allows any type.
; 2. Inside <...>, single capitals stand for types: T, U and E for any type,
;    F for a function type. They are placeholders in this file, not type
;    parameters of the language like the T in [Option T].
; 3. X... means zero or more X. X X... means one or more.
; 4. X? means X may be left out.
; 5. Everything else is literal syntax, including [ ], | | and ->.
; 6. -> T written after a complete form gives the type of the whole form. It
;    is not part of the syntax: (do) -> Unit means (do) is a Unit value.

;;; 0.3 example annotations
; 1. Examples are fragments. A define, struct or enum example sits at the
;    top level. Any other example reads as part of the body of main (9.1).
;    Earlier examples in the same entry are in scope.
; 2. A trailing comment states the outcome:
;      returns X     the example evaluates to X
;      type: T       the example has type T
;      name : T      the example binds name with type T
;      prints: X     the example writes X to the console
;      warning: ...  the example compiles, with this warning
;      error: ...    the example does not compile
;      panics: ...   the example compiles, then stops at run time (8.8)
; 3. A tag such as [5.5.3] at the end of the comment names the rule the
;    example exercises.
; 4. A comment starting with hypothetical: marks an example that relies on
;    something this file does not define yet. Each one is listed in 11.9.

;;; 0.4 reasons
; 1. A line starting with why: gives a reason the author stated.
; 2. A line starting with why?: gives a reason added during the rewrite and
;    not yet confirmed. Confirm it by deleting the ?, or delete the line.

;;;; 1 lexical rules

;;; 1.1 comments
; 1. A comment starts with ; and runs to the end of the line. The compiler
;    ignores it.
; 2. The number of semicolons at the start of a comment sets its level.
;    Levels are for readers and editors, for headers and folding. They never
;    change meaning.
; 3. A comment starting with exactly ;: is a doc comment. One starting with
;    ;;: is an ordinary comment. What a doc comment attaches to is open
;    (11.1.6).

(+ 1 2)  ; a comment after code  [1.1.1]
;: a doc comment  [1.1.3]
; this is a level-one comment; at the start of a line, ;; begins a level-two
; comment, which this file uses as a sub-entry header  [1.1.2]

;;; 1.2 tokens and whitespace
; 1. Whitespace separates tokens. It is optional next to the delimiters
;    ( ) [ ] { } | and next to :.
; 2. -> is always a token of its own, even between letters.
; 3. A token that starts with - followed by a digit is a number literal.
; 4. A line break is whitespace, except that it ends a comment.

(+ x y)      ; same as ( + x y )                   [1.2.1]
|x: u32|     ; same as |x : u32|                   [1.2.1]
string->u32  ; three tokens: string -> u32         [1.2.2]
-5           ; one token, a literal                [1.2.3]
(- 5)        ; a call of - on 5; returns -5 (8.2)  [1.2.3]
(+ 1
   2)  ; the line break is just whitespace; returns 3  [1.2.4]

;;; 1.3 literals

;; 1.3.1 numbers
; 1. An integer literal is one or more digits, optionally preceded by -.
; 2. A float literal is digits, a ., and more digits, optionally preceded by
;    -.
; 3. A number literal has no type suffix. Its type comes from where it is
;    used (5.1.1). Other formats are open (11.1.2).

42           ; an integer literal      [1.3.1.1]
-7           ; an integer literal      [1.3.1.1]
3.14         ; a float literal         [1.3.1.2]
(the u8 42)  ; 42 typed as a u8 (8.6)  [1.3.1.3]

;; 1.3.2 booleans and unit
; 1. true and false are the two bool literals.
; 2. () is the literal for the single value of Unit (3.2).

true  ; type: bool  [1.3.2.1]
()    ; type: Unit  [1.3.2.2]

;; 1.3.3 characters
; 1. A char literal is a single character between single quotes: 'c'.
; 2. Single quotes never delimit strings.

'c'      ; type: char                       [1.3.3.1]
'hello'  ; error: ' delimits a single char  [1.3.3.2]

;; 1.3.4 strings
; 1. A simple string is written between double quotes: "hello world!".
; 2. A fancy string is written between backticks: `hello ${name}`. It may
;    contain ' and " without escapes.
; 3. Inside a fancy string, ${<Identifier>} marks a parameter.
; 4. A fancy string is not a string. It is a function value (5.1.2).

"hello world!"        ; type: string                   [1.3.4.1]
`say "hi" and 'bye'`  ; ' and " need no escapes        [1.3.4.2]
`hello ${name}`       ; name is a parameter            [1.3.4.3]
`hello world!`        ; type: [-> string], not string  [1.3.4.4]

;; 1.3.5 escapes
; 1. A backslash starts an escape. Simple strings, fancy strings and char
;    literals support the same escapes:
;      \n  newline         \t  tab           \\  backslash
;      \`  backtick        \$  dollar sign   \"  double quote
;      \'  single quote
;    Any other escape is open (11.1.4).

`\nthis is a newline, and this is a backtick: \`!`  ; \n and \` in a fancy string  [1.3.5.1]
"she said \"hi\"\n"  ; \" and \n in a simple string                                [1.3.5.1]
'\''                 ; a char holding a single quote                               [1.3.5.1]
`costs \${price}`    ; \$ stops ${ from marking a parameter; type: [-> string]     [1.3.5.1]

;;; 1.4 naming conventions
; 1. PascalCase names types, enum variants and constructors: Employee,
;    Weapon.Bow.
; 2. snake_case names values and functions: add_one, person_1.
; 3. SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE names constants. A constant is a define whose type
;    is a primitive type (3.1): PI, SOME_NUMBER.
; 4. Breaking 1 to 3 is a warning, not an error.

(struct Employee |name : string|)  ; a type                                           [1.4.1]
(define add_one = {fn |x : u32 -> u32| (+ x 1)})  ; a function                        [1.4.2]
(define PI : f64 = 3.14)           ; an f64 constant                                  [1.4.3]
(define GREETING : string = "hi")  ; string is primitive                              [1.4.3]
(define pi : f64 = 3.14)           ; warning: constants are SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE      [1.4.4]
(struct employee |name : string|)  ; warning: types are PascalCase                    [1.4.4]
(define AddOne = {fn |x : u32 -> u32| (+ x 1)})  ; warning: functions are snake_case  [1.4.4]

;;;; 2 grammar

;;; 2.1 token classes
Keyword      ; a reserved word (2.2)
Identifier   ; a name: x, first_or, Employee
Literal      ; a literal value (1.3)
Punctuation  ; a punctuation token (2.3)
Delimiter    ; ( ) { } [ ] or | | (2.4)
; 1. Every token belongs to exactly one of these classes.
; 2. A qualified name such as Weapon.Bow is two identifiers joined by the
;    punctuation token . (2.3.1).

(define PI : f64 = 3.14)  ; each token has one class; see 2.6.1  [2.1.1]
Weapon.Bow                ; Identifier, Punctuation, Identifier  [2.1.2]

;;; 2.2 keywords
do      ; a block (5.3)
fn      ; a function value (5.4)
forall  ; the type parameters of a generic function (7.3)
define  ; a global binding (4.2)
let     ; a local binding (4.3)
if      ; a two-way choice (5.5), and the start of a guard (5.7.1)
when    ; a Unit body run if a condition holds (5.6)
unless  ; a Unit body run unless a condition holds (5.6)
struct  ; a struct type (4.4)
enum    ; an enum type (4.5)
match   ; pattern matching (5.7)
next    ; an arm that shares the following arm's value (5.7.3)
loop    ; a loop (5.8)
recur   ; a new pass of a loop or fn (5.9)
for     ; a counting loop (5.10)
mod     ; imports a module (9.3)
export  ; exports a define, enum, or struct (9.4)
; 1. Keywords are reserved and are not values. A keyword cannot be bound,
;    passed or returned; it can only be the head of its own form.
; 2. The built-ins print, panic, assert and debug are not values either.
;    They are special forms (8.1.2).

(define choose = if)  ; error: if is a keyword, not a value  [2.2.1]
(let do : u32 = 1)    ; error: do is reserved                [2.2.1]
(define say = print)  ; error: print is a special form       [2.2.2]

;;; 2.3 punctuation
.   ; qualified names, field access and literal indexing
:   ; type annotation
::  ; module access (9.3)
->  ; separates inputs from the output in signatures and function types
=>  ; separates a match arm's pattern from its value (5.7.1)
_   ; the wildcard pattern (6.1)
@   ; binds a whole value in a pattern (6.6)
..  ; skips items in a pattern, or copies fields in construction (4.4.4)
=   ; binds: define, let, defaults, bindings, field patterns
;   ; starts a comment (1.1)
; 1. A . between two identifiers forms a qualified name: Weapon.Bow,
;    Option.Some, Employee.dept.
; 2. name.field, where name is bound to a struct, reads that field (4.4.3).
; 3. name.N, where name is bound to a list and N is an integer literal, reads
;    element N (3.4.2).
; 4. The left side of . must be an identifier. For any other value, call an
;    accessor function (4.4.3) or get (8.7.2).

; with jane : Employee, numbers : [List i32], and make_employee : [-> Employee]
Weapon.Bow                       ; a variant name                                [2.3.1]
Employee.dept                    ; an accessor name; type: [Employee -> string]  [2.3.1]
jane.dept                        ; reads jane's dept field                       [2.3.2]
numbers.0                        ; reads element 0 of numbers                    [2.3.3]
(make_employee).dept             ; error: . must follow an identifier            [2.3.4]
(Employee.dept (make_employee))  ; reads the field of any Employee value         [2.3.4]

;;; 2.4 delimiters

;; 2.4.1 what each delimiter wraps
()  ; an expression whose value is not a function (2.4.2)
{}  ; an expression whose value is a function (2.4.2)
[]  ; a type, a type header (7.2) or a forall list (7.3)
||  ; a signature or a field list (2.5.2)
; 1. [ ] never wrap an expression, and ( ) and { } never wrap a type.
; 2. Bars pair up within one level of brackets. A | | pair inside a nested
;    { } belongs to the form inside those braces, which is how a fn can
;    appear inside a signature (2.6.3).

(let xs : [List u32] = (list 1 2))  ; a type in [ ], a value in ( )         [2.4.1.1]
(let xs : (List u32) = (list 1 2))  ; error: a type must be in [ ]          [2.4.1.1]
[list 1 2]                          ; error: [ ] cannot wrap an expression  [2.4.1.1]
{fn |f : [u32 -> u32] = {fn |y : u32 -> u32| y} -> u32| (f 1)}
                                    ; two bar pairs, kept apart by { }  [2.4.1.2]

;; 2.4.2 ( ) versus { }
; 1. A form is written in { } when its value is a function, and in ( )
;    otherwise. This applies to calls and to every keyword form.
; 2. What counts is the type known at that point in the code. A value whose
;    type is a type parameter counts as not a function, even if that
;    parameter could stand for a function type (7.3.4).
; 3. Using the wrong bracket is a compile error.
; 4. {f} calls f with no arguments and expects a function back. It does not
;    wrap f. A function value on its own is written bare: f.
; 5. fn always produces a function, so it is always written {fn ...}.
;    why?: a reader can tell from the bracket alone whether an expression
;    produces a function

(define make_adder = {fn |x : u32 -> [u32 -> u32]| {fn |y : u32 -> u32| (+ x y)}})
{make_adder 1}           ; type: [u32 -> u32]                                            [2.4.2.1]
({make_adder 1} 2)       ; returns 3; the callee can be any function value               [2.4.2.1]
{if true {make_adder 1} {make_adder 2}}  ; type: [u32 -> u32]; if follows the same rule  [2.4.2.1]
(make_adder 1)           ; error: the call returns a function; write {make_adder 1}      [2.4.2.3]
{+ 1 2}                  ; error: the call returns an i32; write (+ 1 2)                 [2.4.2.3]
make_adder               ; type: [u32 -> [u32 -> u32]], written bare                     [2.4.2.4]
{make_adder}             ; error: make_adder needs its argument x                        [2.4.2.4]
(define greet = `hello`)
(greet)                  ; returns "hello"; a call with no arguments                     [2.4.2.4]
(fn |x : u32 -> u32| x)  ; error: fn makes a function; write {fn ...}                    [2.4.2.5]

(define identity = {fn [forall T] |x : T -> T| x})
(identity 5)               ; returns 5; T is i32                                           [2.4.2.2]
{identity {make_adder 1}}  ; type: [u32 -> u32]; at this call T is known to be a function  [2.4.2.2]
(identity {make_adder 1})
    ; error: here T is [u32 -> u32], so the call returns a function  [2.4.2.2]

;;; 2.5 syntactic categories
; These categories appear in general forms throughout the file.

;; 2.5.1 values and expressions
<Special T>      ; a form whose head is a keyword (2.2) or a built-in special form (8.1.2)
<Declaration>    ; a <Special Unit> that binds a name: define, let, struct, enum
<Callee T>       ; a <Value F> whose return type is T: the function being called
<Application T>  ; (<Callee T> <Value>...), or {<Callee T> <Value>...} when T is a function type
<Expression T>   ; a <Special T> or an <Application T>
<Value T>        ; an <Expression T>, a literal of type T, or an identifier bound to a T
; 1. Every <Declaration> returns Unit, so it is an <Expression Unit> and a
;    <Value Unit>.
; 2. Both function values and other values are <Value T>s. An identifier
;    bound to a function is a <Value F>.

(let x : u32 = 1)  ; a <Declaration>; returns ()                                     [2.5.1.1]
(+ 1 2)            ; an <Application i32>, so an <Expression i32> and a <Value i32>  [2.5.1.2]
+                  ; an identifier bound to a function: a <Value F>                  [2.5.1.2]

;; 2.5.2 types, parameters and fields
<Type>           ; a type identifier such as u32, or a bracketed type such as [List u32]
<Annotated T>    ; <Identifier> : <Type>
<Parameter T>    ; <Annotated T>, or <Annotated T> = <Value T> with a default
<Fields>         ; |<Parameter>...|: parameters with no output
<Signature>      ; |<Parameter>... -> <Type>|: parameters and an output
<Definition>     ; <Identifier> <Fields>?: a struct, or an enum variant
<Binding>        ; <Identifier> = <Value>: a value given to a field or parameter by name
<Constructor T>  ; (<Callee T> <Binding>...): builds a struct or variant value of type T
<Type Header>    ; [<Identifier> <Identifier> <Identifier>...]: a generic type and its parameters
<Forall>         ; [forall <Identifier> <Identifier>...]: a generic function's type parameters
; 1. A <Signature> always has an output type after ->. A bar list with no ->
;    is always a <Fields>.
; 2. A type identifier is read as a <Type> wherever a type is expected,
;    even though it is also an Identifier.

{fn |x : u32 -> u32| x}   ; |x : u32 -> u32| is a <Signature>                           [2.5.2.1]
{fn |x : u32| x}          ; error: fn needs a <Signature>, and |x : u32| has no output  [2.5.2.1]
(struct Point |x : i32 y : i32|)  ; |x : i32 y : i32| is a <Fields>                     [2.5.2.1]
(struct Pair |x : i32 -> i32|)  ; error: struct takes a <Fields>, which has no output   [2.5.2.1]
(define E : f64 = 2.718)  ; f64 is read as a <Type>                                     [2.5.2.2]

;; 2.5.3 arms and patterns
<Pattern T>      ; a pattern that matches values of type T (6)
<Field Pattern>  ; <Identifier> = <Pattern>: a field, and the pattern its value must match
<Guard>          ; if <Value bool>
<Arm T U>        ; <Pattern T> <Guard>? => <Value U>, or <Pattern T> <Guard>? => next

;;; 2.6 worked breakdowns
; Each diagram takes one expression apart. Every line under the first repeats
; some of its tokens in the same columns.

;; 2.6.1 a constant definition
(define PI : f64 = 3.14)  ; the whole form: a <Declaration>, so an <Expression Unit>
 define                   ; Keyword
        PI                ; Identifier; after this define, also a <Value f64>
           :              ; Punctuation
             f64          ; a <Type>; also an Identifier, but <Type> wins (2.5.2.2)
        PI : f64          ; <Annotated f64>
                 =        ; Punctuation
                   3.14   ; Literal, and a <Value f64>

;; 2.6.2 an anonymous function
{fn |x : u32 y : u32 -> u32| (+ x y)}  ; <Expression [u32 u32 -> u32]>
 fn                                    ; Keyword
    |x : u32 y : u32 -> u32|           ; <Signature>; in the body, x and y are <Value u32>s
     x       y                         ; Identifiers
       :       :     ->                ; Punctuation
         u32     u32    u32            ; <Type>s
     x : u32                           ; <Annotated u32>
             y : u32                   ; <Annotated u32>
                             (+ x y)   ; <Expression u32>, so a <Value u32>
                              +        ; Identifier; here a function of type [u32 u32 -> u32]
                                x y    ; Identifiers, and <Value u32>s

;; 2.6.3 a function-valued default
; The default for f is itself a fn, so the signature holds a second pair of
; bars. The { } around the inner fn keep the two pairs apart (2.4.1.2).
{fn |x : u32  f : [u32 -> u32] = {fn |y : u32 -> u32| (+ y 1)}  -> u32| (f x)}
;   ^ open (depth 1)                 ^ open (depth 2)                 ^ close (depth 1)
;                                                   ^ close (depth 2)

;; 2.6.4 a match arm
(Weapon.Bow damage = d) if (> d 10.0) => true  ; <Arm Weapon bool>
(Weapon.Bow damage = d)                        ; <Pattern Weapon>: a constructor pattern (6.4)
 Weapon.Bow                                    ; Identifier: the variant, qualified (6.4.2)
            damage = d                         ; <Field Pattern>: the field damage, bound to d
                        if (> d 10.0)          ; <Guard>; d is bound here (5.7.1.2)
                                      =>       ; Punctuation
                                         true  ; <Value bool>: the arm's value

;; 2.6.5 a loop
(loop |n : u32 = 1 -> u32| (if (< n 9) (recur n = (* n 2)) n))  ; <Expression u32>; returns 16
 loop                                                           ; Keyword
      |n : u32 = 1 -> u32|                                      ; <Signature>
       n : u32 = 1                                              ; <Parameter u32>; n starts at 1
                   -> u32                                       ; the loop's type (5.8.2)
                           (if (< n 9) (recur n = (* n 2)) n)   ; the body
                               (< n 9)                          ; go on while n < 9
                                       (recur n = (* n 2))      ; next pass, n doubled
                                              n = (* n 2)       ; <Binding>
                                                           n    ; the value of the last pass

;; 2.6.6 a generic function
{fn [forall T U] |x : T f : [T -> U] -> U| (f x)}  ; type: [[forall T U] T [T -> U] -> U]
 fn                                                ; Keyword
    [forall T U]                                   ; <Forall>
     forall                                        ; Keyword
            T U                                    ; Identifiers, usable as types from here on
                 |x : T f : [T -> U] -> U|         ; <Signature>
                            [T -> U]               ; <Type>: a function type (3.3)
                                           (f x)   ; <Application U>, in ( ) (2.4.2.2)

;;;; 3 types

;;; 3.1 primitive types
u8      ; unsigned 8-bit integer
u16     ; unsigned 16-bit integer
u32     ; unsigned 32-bit integer
u64     ; unsigned 64-bit integer
i8      ; signed 8-bit integer
i16     ; signed 16-bit integer
i32     ; signed 32-bit integer
i64     ; signed 64-bit integer
f32     ; 32-bit float
f64     ; 64-bit float
bool    ; true or false
char    ; a character, written 'c' (1.3.3)
string  ; text, written "like this" (1.3.4)
; 1. Primitive type names are lowercase.
; 2. The integer and float types are the numerical types.
; 3. There are no implicit conversions. A value changes type only through an
;    explicit cast (8.6).
;    why: every change of type is visible in the code
; 128-bit ("quad") and arbitrary-size ("big") numbers are planned (11.3.1).

(let count : u32 = 3)               ; u32 is primitive, so lowercase               [3.1.1]
(+ 2.5 1.5)                         ; f64 is numerical; returns 4.0                [3.1.2]
(+ true false)                      ; error: + needs numerical or string operands  [3.1.2]
(let wide : u64 = count)            ; error: count is a u32; write (as u64 count)  [3.1.3]
(let wider : u64 = (as u64 count))  ; the explicit cast                            [3.1.3]

;;; 3.2 Unit and Never
Unit   ; a type with exactly one value, written ()
Never  ; a type with no values
; 1. () is the value of an empty expression, such as (do) (5.3).
; 2. Discarding a Unit value never warns. Discarding a value of any other
;    type warns (5.3.4).
;    why: a discarded non-Unit value was computed for nothing, which is
;    usually a mistake
; 3. No expression of type Never produces a value. panic returns Never
;    (8.8). Whether Never fits where another type is expected is open
;    (11.3.4).

()                  ; type: Unit                                  [3.2.1]
(do)                ; returns ()                                  [3.2.1]
(do (print "a") 1)  ; print's () is discarded without a warning   [3.2.2]
(do (+ 1 2) 1)      ; warning: the i32 from (+ 1 2) is discarded  [3.2.2]
(panic "stop")      ; type: Never; panics: stop                   [3.2.3]

;;; 3.3 function types
[T... -> U]  ; a function from parameters of types T... to a U
; 1. A bracketed type whose outermost level contains -> is a function type.
; 2. A function has exactly one output type.
; 3. A value of a function type is produced in { } (2.4.2).
; 4. [-> Unit] takes nothing and returns Unit. It is the emptiest function
;    type.
; 5. The type of a generic function starts with its forall list:
;    [[forall T] T -> T] (7.3).

[u32 u32 -> u32]                ; two u32 parameters, a u32 output                           [3.3.1]
[u32 -> [u32 -> u32]]           ; returns a function                                         [3.3.1]
[[u32 -> u32] u32 -> u32]       ; takes a function as its first parameter                    [3.3.1]
[List [u32 -> u32]]             ; not a function type: the -> is not at the outermost level  [3.3.1]
[u32 -> u32 bool]               ; error: a function has one output type                      [3.3.2]
(if true {fn |-> u32| 1} {fn |-> u32| 2})  ; error: the value is a [-> u32]; write {if ...}  [3.3.3]
{fn |-> Unit| ()}               ; type: [-> Unit]                                            [3.3.4]
{fn [forall T] |x : T -> T| x}  ; type: [[forall T] T -> T]                                  [3.3.5]

;;; 3.4 List

;; 3.4.1 the type
[List T]  ; an ordered list of values of type T
; 1. All elements of a list have the same type T.
; 2. Indices are u32 and start at 0.
; 3. list builds a list (8.7.1), and get reads an element safely (8.7.2).

(let numbers : [List i32] = (list 1 2 3))  ; three i32s                   [3.4.1.1]
(list 1 "two")   ; error: an i32 and a string in one list                 [3.4.1.1]
(get numbers 0)  ; returns (Option.Some value = 1); index 0 is the first  [3.4.1.2]
(let j : i32 = 1)
(get numbers j)  ; error: j is an i32, and an index is a u32              [3.4.1.2]
(get numbers 2)  ; returns (Option.Some value = 3)                        [3.4.1.3]

;; 3.4.2 literal indexing
; 1. name.N, where name is an identifier bound to a list and N is an integer
;    literal, returns element N.
; 2. If N is out of range, name.N panics.
; 3. N must be a literal. For any other index, use get (8.7.2).

numbers.0        ; returns 1                                                    [3.4.2.1]
numbers.2        ; returns 3                                                    [3.4.2.1]
numbers.3        ; panics: index out of range                                   [3.4.2.2]
(let i : u32 = 1)
numbers.i        ; error: numbers.i is field access, and a list has no field i  [3.4.2.3]
(get numbers i)  ; returns (Option.Some value = 2)                              [3.4.2.3]
(list 7 8 9).0   ; error: . must follow an identifier (2.3.4)                   [3.4.2.1]

;;; 3.5 Map (todo)
[Map K V]  ; a map from keys of type K to values of type V
; Maps cannot be built or read yet (11.3.6).

;;; 3.6 Option and Result
[Option T]    ; either a T or nothing
[Result T E]  ; either a success T or an error E
; Both are built-in generic enums (4.5, 7.2), defined as if by:
(enum [Option T]   Some |value : T|   None)
(enum [Result T E] Ok |value : T|     Error |error : E|)
; 1. Their variants are qualified like any other: Option.Some, Option.None,
;    Result.Ok and Result.Error (4.5.2).
; 2. Option.None has no fields, so it is a value, not a function.

(Option.Some value = 5)                     ; type: [Option i32]                      [3.6.1]
(let nothing : [Option i32] = Option.None)  ; no parentheses                          [3.6.2]
(let failed : [Result u32 string] = (Result.Error error = "bad input"))  ; qualified  [3.6.1]
(Some value = 5)                            ; error: write Option.Some                [3.6.1]
(Option.None)                               ; error: Option.None is not a function    [3.6.2]

;;;; 4 declarations

;;; 4.1 bindings and scope
; 1. A binding gives a name to a value. Bindings are immutable: nothing in
;    the language changes a binding once it is made.
; 2. define binds in the global scope. Global definitions are
;    order-independent: each may refer to any other, wherever it appears.
; 3. let binds inside a block. Its name is visible from the next item of the
;    block to the end of the block.
; 4. When a block ends, the names it bound go out of scope, and their values
;    are dropped unless something still refers to them (9.2.2).

(define is_even = {fn |n : u32 -> bool| (if (== n 0) true (is_odd (- n 1)))})
(define is_odd = {fn |n : u32 -> bool| (if (== n 0) false (is_even (- n 1)))})
(is_even 4)  ; returns true; is_even uses is_odd before its define  [4.1.2]
(do
    (let a : u32 = 1)
    (let b : u32 = (+ a 1))  ; a is visible here  [4.1.3]
    b)                       ; returns 2
(do
    (let b : u32 = (+ a 1))  ; error: a is not bound yet  [4.1.3]
    (let a : u32 = 1)
    b)
(do
    (do (let inner : u32 = 1))
    inner)  ; error: inner went out of scope with its block  [4.1.4]
(do
    (let total : u32 = 1)
    (= total 2)  ; error: there is no assignment; = only appears inside binding forms  [4.1.1]
    total)

;;; 4.2 define
(define <Annotated T> = <Value T>) -> Unit
(define <Identifier> = <Value T>) -> Unit
; 1. define binds a name in the global scope. It may only appear at the top
;    level.
; 2. The annotation may be left out only when the value is a literal or a fn
;    literal. The name then has that literal's type.

(define SOME_NUMBER : u32 = (+ 2 5))  ; returns (); SOME_NUMBER is global            [4.2.1]
(do (define X : u32 = 1) X)           ; error: define only appears at the top level  [4.2.1]
(define E = 2.718)                    ; E : f64, from the literal                    [4.2.2]
(define add = {fn |x : u32 y : u32 -> u32| (+ x y)})  ; add : [u32 u32 -> u32]       [4.2.2]
; an annotation is allowed even where it could be left out  [4.2.2]
(define add_2 : [u32 u32 -> u32] = {fn |x : u32 y : u32 -> u32| (+ x y)})
(define LIMIT = (+ 2 5))        ; error: (+ 2 5) is not a literal, so annotate it  [4.2.2]
(define primes = (list 2 3 5))  ; error: a call is not a literal, so annotate it   [4.2.2]

;;; 4.3 let
(let <Annotated T> = <Value T>) -> Unit
(let <Identifier> = <Value T>) -> Unit
; 1. let binds a name for the rest of the enclosing block (4.1.3).
; 2. As with define, the annotation may be left out only when the value is a
;    literal or a fn literal (4.2.2).
; 3. let at the top level is legal, but it is an anti-pattern: use define.
;    why?: a top-level define can be exported and used before the line it is
;    on, so a top-level let only adds an ordering constraint

(do
    (let some_other_number : u32 = (+ 2 5))    ; annotated           [4.3.1]
    (let greeting = "hi")                      ; greeting : string   [4.3.2]
    (let inc = {fn |x : u32 -> u32| (+ x 1)})  ; inc : [u32 -> u32]  [4.3.2]
    (inc some_other_number))                   ; returns 8
(do (let doubled = (* 2 3)) doubled)  ; error: (* 2 3) is not a literal, so annotate it  [4.3.2]
(let top_level_number : u32 = 7)      ; legal at the top level, but use define           [4.3.3]

;;; 4.4 struct

;; 4.4.1 definition
(struct <Definition>) -> Unit
(struct <Type Header> <Fields>) -> Unit  ; a generic struct (7.2)
; 1. A struct is a product type: each value holds all of its fields.
; 2. The definition's <Identifier> becomes a type, and the name of that
;    type's constructor.
; 3. Every field is a named <Parameter>, and may have a default value.

(struct Employee |
    name   : string
    age    : u32
    dept   : string = "unknown"  ; a field with a default                    [4.4.1.3]
    salary : f64|)               ; Employee is now a type and a constructor  [4.4.1.2]
(struct Hacker |
    skill  : string
    salary : f64|)
(struct Sword |
    damage    : f64
    name      : string
    legendary : bool|)
(struct Point |x : i32 y : i32|)  ; every Point holds both an x and a y  [4.4.1.1]
(struct Bad |i32 string|)         ; error: every field needs a name      [4.4.1.3]

;; 4.4.2 construction
(<Identifier> <Binding>...) -> T
; 1. A struct value is built by calling the struct's name with one binding
;    per field.
; 2. Bindings are by name, so their order does not matter.
; 3. A field with a default may be left out, and then takes its default.
; 4. It is a compile error to leave out a field that has no default, to bind
;    a field twice, or to bind a name that is not a field.

(let john : Employee = (Employee
    name = "john"
    age = 24
    dept = "finance"
    salary = 2.0))  ; one binding per field  [4.4.2.1]
(let jane : Employee = (Employee
    name = "jane"
    age = 22
    salary = 2.5))  ; dept is left out, so it is "unknown"  [4.4.2.3]
(define excalibur : Sword = (Sword
    damage = 12.0
    legendary = true
    name = "Excalibur"))  ; not in definition order, which is fine  [4.4.2.2]
(Point 1 2)                       ; error: fields are bound by name                     [4.4.2.1]
(Employee name = "max" age = 30)  ; error: salary has no default                        [4.4.2.4]
(Employee name = "max" name = "m" age = 30 salary = 1.0)  ; error: name is bound twice  [4.4.2.4]
(Point x = 1 y = 2 z = 3)         ; error: Point has no field z                         [4.4.2.4]

;; 4.4.3 field access
; 1. Every field F of a struct S has an accessor function S.F of type
;    [S -> T], where T is the field's type. The struct definition creates
;    it, with the same scope as the struct.
; 2. name.F reads field F of the struct bound to the identifier name.
; 3. . only works after an identifier (2.3.4). For any other value, call
;    the accessor function.
;    why?: . then stays part of names and is never an operator on
;    expressions

jane.dept             ; returns "unknown"                                          [4.4.3.2]
excalibur.legendary   ; returns true                                               [4.4.3.2]
jane.rank             ; error: Employee has no field rank                          [4.4.3.2]
(Employee.dept jane)  ; returns "unknown"                                          [4.4.3.1]
Employee.dept         ; type: [Employee -> string]                                 [4.4.3.1]
(let get_name : [Employee -> string] = Employee.name)
(get_name john)       ; returns "john"; an accessor is an ordinary function value  [4.4.3.1]
(Employee
    name = "jane"
    age = 22
    salary = 2.5).dept  ; error: . must follow an identifier  [4.4.3.3]
(Employee.dept (Employee
    name = "jane"
    age = 22
    salary = 2.5))  ; returns "unknown"  [4.4.3.3]

;; 4.4.4 copying fields
(<Identifier> ..<Identifier> <Binding>...) -> T
; 1. ..source copies every field of source, which is a value of the same
;    struct type.
; 2. A binding after ..source replaces the copied value of that field.

(let bob : Employee = (Employee
    ..jane
    name = "bob"
    age = 23))  ; name and age replaced  [4.4.4.2]
bob.salary                                   ; returns 2.5, copied from jane             [4.4.4.1]
(let jane_2 : Employee = (Employee ..jane))  ; a field-for-field copy                    [4.4.4.1]
(Hacker ..jane skill = "none")               ; error: jane is an Employee, not a Hacker  [4.4.4.1]

;;; 4.5 enum

;; 4.5.1 definition
(enum <Identifier> <Definition>...) -> Unit
(enum <Type Header> <Definition>...) -> Unit  ; a generic enum (7.2)
; 1. An enum is a sum type: each value is exactly one of its variants.
; 2. The <Identifier> becomes a type. Each <Definition> becomes a variant,
;    named Enum.Variant.
; 3. A variant's fields follow the struct rules (4.4.1.3): every field is
;    named, and may have a default.
; 4. A variant with no fields is a value, not a function.

(enum Weapon
    Sword |sword : Sword|
    Bow |damage : f64|
    Fist)  ; variants Weapon.Sword, Weapon.Bow and Weapon.Fist  [4.5.1.2]
(enum Person
    Employee |person : Employee|
    Hacker |person : Hacker|)  ; a Person is an Employee or a Hacker, never both  [4.5.1.1]
(enum Signal
    Beep |pitch : u32 = 440|
    Silence)  ; a variant field with a default  [4.5.1.3]
(enum Broken
    Wrapped |f64|)  ; error: the field needs a name  [4.5.1.3]

;; 4.5.2 construction
(<Identifier>.<Identifier> <Binding>...) -> T
<Identifier>.<Identifier>  ; a variant with no fields
; 1. A variant with fields is built like a struct (4.4.2), with the
;    qualified name Enum.Variant as the callee.
; 2. A variant with no fields is written Enum.Variant, without parentheses.
; 3. A variant's name is always qualified with its enum's name.

(define switchbow : Weapon = (Weapon.Bow damage = 5.0))  ; built like a struct      [4.5.2.1]
(define legend : Weapon = (Weapon.Sword sword = excalibur))
    ; the variant Weapon.Sword holds the struct Sword  [4.5.2.3]
(define bare_hands : Weapon = Weapon.Fist)  ; a value  [4.5.1.4, 4.5.2.2]
(Signal.Beep)                               ; a Beep with pitch 440                 [4.5.2.1]
(Weapon.Fist)                               ; error: Weapon.Fist is not a function  [4.5.2.2]
(Bow damage = 5.0)                          ; error: write Weapon.Bow               [4.5.2.3]
(do
    (let person_1 : Person = (Person.Employee person = john))
    (let person_2 : Person = (Person.Hacker person = (Hacker
        skill = "backdoors"
        salary = 1.0)))
    (print (to_string person_2)))  ; prints person_2 as text (11.7.6)  [4.5.2.1]

;;;; 5 expressions

;;; 5.1 literals

;; 5.1.1 number literals
; 1. A number literal takes its type from the type expected where it
;    appears. In (+ x 1) with x : u8, the 1 is a u8.
; 2. When no type is expected, an integer literal is an i32, or an i64 if it
;    is too large for an i32. A float literal is an f64.
; 3. (the <Type> <Literal>) states a literal's type directly (8.6).

(let small : u8 = 200)   ; 200 is a u8                       [5.1.1.1]
(+ small 1)              ; the 1 is a u8; returns 201, a u8  [5.1.1.1]
(let ratio : f32 = 0.5)  ; 0.5 is an f32                     [5.1.1.1]
42                       ; type: i32                         [5.1.1.2]
3000000000               ; type: i64, too large for an i32   [5.1.1.2]
2.5                      ; type: f64                         [5.1.1.2]
(the u8 5)               ; type: u8                          [5.1.1.3]

;; 5.1.2 fancy strings
; 1. A fancy string is a function value. Each distinct ${name} is a
;    parameter, in order of first appearance. A call returns the text with
;    each ${name} replaced by its argument.
; 2. When the same name appears more than once, it is one parameter.
; 3. Every parameter is a string. Convert other values with to_string (8.6).
; 4. ${name} names a parameter. It never reads a binding called name from
;    the surrounding scope.
; 5. A fancy string with no ${} has type [-> string].
;    why: the fn it stands for is "extremely messy", which is why the
;    sugar hides it

`name: ${name}, number: ${num}`  ; type: [string string -> string]  [5.1.2.1]
; is equivalent to
{fn |name : string num : string -> string| (+ (+ "name: " name) (+ ", number: " num))}
(`name: ${name}, number: ${num}` "jane" "22")  ; returns "name: jane, number: 22"       [5.1.2.1]
(`${b} before ${a}` "1" "2")                   ; returns "1 before 2"; b appears first  [5.1.2.1]
`${a} and ${a} again`                          ; type: [string -> string]               [5.1.2.2]
(`${a} and ${a} again` "x")                    ; returns "x and x again"                [5.1.2.2]
(`count: ${n}` 5)                              ; error: parameters are strings          [5.1.2.3]
(`count: ${n}` (to_string 5))                  ; returns "count: 5"                     [5.1.2.3]
(do
    (let name : string = "jane")
    (`hi ${name}`))  ; error: missing argument; ${name} is a parameter  [5.1.2.4]
`hello`    ; type: [-> string]  [5.1.2.5]
(`hello`)  ; returns "hello"    [5.1.2.5]

;;; 5.2 calls
(<Callee T> <Value>...) -> T
{<Callee T> <Value>...} -> T  ; when T is a function type
; 1. A call evaluates its callee and its arguments, then applies the callee
;    to the arguments. The order of evaluation is open (11.5.1).
; 2. Arguments bind to parameters from left to right. A function has a
;    fixed number of parameters, and passing more is a compile error.
; 3. Trailing parameters that have defaults may be left out, and take their
;    defaults. Leaving out a parameter with no default is a compile error.
; 4. The built-in - is the one exception: called with one argument, its
;    first parameter takes the default 0 (8.2.2).
; 5. A built-in that works on several types takes its concrete type from
;    its arguments (8.1.3).

; with x, y, z : i32
(+ x y)        ; x + y                                      [5.2.1]
(+ (+ x y) z)  ; x + y + z                                  [5.2.1]
(+ x y z)      ; error: + takes two arguments               [5.2.2]
(shl 5)        ; returns 10; n takes its default 1          [5.2.3]
(shl 5 2)      ; returns 20                                 [5.2.3]
(- 5)          ; returns -5, the same as (- 0 5)            [5.2.4]
(+ 1 2)        ; + on i32s; returns 3                       [5.2.5]
(+ "a" "b")    ; + on strings; returns "ab"                 [5.2.5]
(define pick = {fn |x : u32 = 1 y : u32 -> u32| (+ x y)})
(pick 5 6)     ; returns 11                                 [5.2.2]
(pick 5)       ; error: 5 binds to x, and y has no default  [5.2.3]
(define one = {fn |-> u32| 1})
(one)          ; returns 1; a call with no arguments        [5.2.1]
(one 1)        ; error: one takes no arguments              [5.2.2]

;;; 5.3 do
(do <Value>... <Value T>) -> T
{do <Value>... <Value F>} -> F
(do) -> Unit
; 1. A do block evaluates its items in order.
; 2. The block's value is the value of its last item.
; 3. A let inside the block binds for the items after it, and the binding
;    is dropped when the block ends (4.1).
; 4. Every item but the last is discarded. Discarding a value that is not
;    Unit warns (3.2.2).
; 5. An empty block returns ().

(do
    (let x : u32 = 2)  ; x is in scope from here on           [5.3.3]
    (let y : u32 = 3)  ; x and y are in scope                 [5.3.3]
    (+ x y))           ; returns 5, then x and y are dropped  [5.3.2]
(do
    (print "one")
    (print "two"))  ; prints: one  [5.3.1]
                    ;         two
(do
    (+ 1 2)         ; warning: the i32 is discarded  [5.3.4]
    (print "done")  ; a discarded (), no warning     [5.3.4]
    3)              ; returns 3
(do (let x : u32 = 1))  ; returns (), the value of the let  [5.3.2]
(do)                    ; returns ()                        [5.3.5]
{do
    (let n : u32 = 10)
    {fn |x : u32 -> u32| (+ x n)}}  ; type: [u32 -> u32]; a function-valued block  [2.4.2.1]

;;; 5.4 fn

;; 5.4.1 anonymous functions
{fn <Identifier>? <Forall>? <Signature> <Value T>} -> F
; The <Identifier> names the function (5.4.3). The <Forall> makes it generic
; (7.3).
; 1. fn creates a function value, so it is always written in { } (2.4.2.5).
; 2. The <Signature> lists the parameters and, after ->, the single output
;    type. The output type is required (2.5.2.1).
; 3. The parameters are bound in the body. The body can be any value,
;    function or not, and its type must be the output type.
; 4. The body may also use any binding in scope where the fn is written. The
;    function keeps those values alive (9.2.2).
; 5. The type F of the fn is built from its parameter and output types:
;    |x : u32 y : u32 -> u32| gives [u32 u32 -> u32].

{fn |x : u32 y : u32 -> u32| (+ x y)}  ; a simple adder, like + on u32s
{                                   }  ; a fn is a function value, so it is wrapped in { }
 fn                                   ; it starts with the keyword fn
    |                      |          ; its parameters and output are wrapped in | |
     x : u32 y : u32                  ; each parameter is a name, a : and a type, with no separators
                     -> u32           ; -> and the one output type
                             (+ x y)  ; the body: what the function computes and returns

({fn |x : u32 y : u32 -> u32| (+ x y)} 2 3)  ; returns 5; a fn called directly         [5.4.1.1]
{fn |-> Unit| ()}                            ; type: [-> Unit]                         [5.4.1.5]
{fn |x : u32 -> u32| (+ x 1)}                ; type: [u32 -> u32]                      [5.4.1.5]
{fn |x : u32 -> string| x}                   ; error: the body is a u32, not a string  [5.4.1.3]
{fn |x : u32| x}                             ; error: the output type is required      [5.4.1.2]
{fn |x : u32 -> [u32 -> u32]| {fn |y : u32 -> u32| y}}  ; a body that is a function    [5.4.1.3]
(define make_adder = {fn |x : u32 -> [u32 -> u32]|
    {fn |y : u32 -> u32| (+ x y)}})  ; the inner fn uses the outer x  [5.4.1.4]
({make_adder 10} 5)  ; returns 15; x = 10 lives on in the inner fn  [5.4.1.4]

;; 5.4.2 default parameters
; 1. A parameter written name : Type = value has a default, used when a call
;    leaves it out (5.2.3).
; 2. A default may be a function value. Its fn brings a second pair of bars
;    into the signature, kept apart by its { } (2.6.3).
;    why: "bracketing allows this to be possible"

(define adder_default = {fn |x : u32 y : u32 = 5 -> u32| (+ x y)})
(adder_default 2)    ; returns 7; y defaults to 5  [5.4.2.1]
(adder_default 2 3)  ; returns 5                   [5.4.2.1]
(adder_default)      ; error: x has no default     [5.2.3]
(define apply_or_inc = {fn |x : u32  f : [u32 -> u32] = {fn |y : u32 -> u32| (+ y 1)}  -> u32|
    (f x)})
(apply_or_inc 1)                  ; returns 2, using the default f  [5.4.2.2]
(apply_or_inc 1 {make_adder 10})  ; returns 11                      [5.4.2.2]

;; 5.4.3 named functions
; 1. In {fn name <Signature> <Value T>}, name is bound to the function
;    itself, and is visible only inside the body.
; 2. The name lets the body call the function itself.
;    why: factorial in the example below is not bound until after the fn is
;    evaluated, so without fact the body could not refer to it
; 3. The name is not bound outside the fn.

(do
    (let factorial = {fn fact |n : u64 -> u64|
        (if (== n 0)
            1
            (* n (fact (- n 1))))})  ; fact is the fn itself  [5.4.3.1]
    (factorial 5))                   ; returns 120            [5.4.3.2]
(do
    (let broken = {fn |n : u64 -> u64|
        (if (== n 0) 1 (* n (broken (- n 1))))})
    (broken 5))  ; error: broken is not bound inside its own value  [5.4.3.2]
(do
    (let factorial = {fn fact |n : u64 -> u64| (if (== n 0) 1 (* n (fact (- n 1))))})
    (fact 5))  ; error: fact is only bound inside the fn  [5.4.3.3]

;;; 5.5 if
(if <Value bool> <Value T> <Value T>) -> T
{if <Value bool> <Value F> <Value F>} -> F
; 1. The condition is a <Value bool>.
; 2. When the condition is true, the if evaluates the first branch and
;    returns its value. Otherwise it evaluates and returns the second. The
;    other branch is never evaluated.
;    why?: the loop in 5.8 and factorial in 5.4.3 only stop because of this
; 3. Both branches have the same type T. A mismatch is a compile error, and
;    no choice of brackets fixes it.
; 4. The braced form is used exactly when T is a function type (2.4.2).

(if (== 5 2) 4 3)                        ; returns 3                                  [5.5.2]
(if true "yes" "no")                     ; returns "yes"                              [5.5.2]
(if (> 3 2) (print "big") (print "small"))  ; prints: big, and only that              [5.5.2]
(if 1 2 3)                               ; error: the condition must be a bool        [5.5.1]
(if true 5 2.5)                          ; error: the branches are an i32 and an f64  [5.5.3]
{if false `hello ${world_name}` "hello world!"}
    ; error: a [string -> string] and a string; neither ( ) nor { } fixes this        [5.5.3]
{if true {make_adder 1} {make_adder 2}}  ; type: [u32 -> u32]                         [5.5.4]
(if true {make_adder 1} {make_adder 2})
    ; error: the branches are functions; write {if ...}  [5.5.4]

;;; 5.6 when and unless
(when <Value bool> <Value Unit>) -> Unit
(unless <Value bool> <Value Unit>) -> Unit
; 1. when evaluates its body only if the condition is true.
; 2. unless evaluates its body only if the condition is false.
; 3. The body is a single <Value Unit>, and the form returns (). There is no
;    else branch.
; 4. (when c v) is the same as (if c v ()), and (unless c v) is the same as
;    (if c () v).

(let x : u32 = 5)
(when (> x 3) (print "x is big"))       ; prints: x is big                                  [5.6.1]
(unless (> x 3) (print "x is small"))   ; returns (), and prints nothing                    [5.6.2]
(when (> x 3) 7)                        ; error: the body must be Unit, not an i32          [5.6.3]
(when (> x 3) (print "a") (print "b"))  ; error: when takes one body; wrap several in a do  [5.6.3]
(when (> x 3) (do
    (print "a")
    (print "b")))  ; prints: a  [5.6.3]
                   ;         b
(if (> x 3) (print "x is big") ())  ; the same as the first when  [5.6.4]

;;; 5.7 match
(match <Value T> <Arm T U>...) -> U
{match <Value T> <Arm T F>...} -> F
; A match compares a value against patterns (6) and evaluates the arm that
; applies.

;; 5.7.1 arms and guards
<Pattern T> <Guard>? => <Value U>
<Pattern T> <Guard>? => next
; 1. Each arm has a pattern, an optional guard, and then a value or next
;    (5.7.3).
; 2. An arm applies when its pattern matches and its guard, if it has one,
;    is true.
; 3. The bindings a pattern makes are visible in that arm's guard and value
;    only.
; 4. The if of a guard is part of the arm, not an if form (5.5).
; 5. Every arm's value has the same type U.
; 6. A binding that the arm never uses warns. Use _ instead (6.1).

(let weapon : Weapon = Weapon.Fist)
(match weapon
    (Weapon.Sword sword = sword)          => sword.legendary  ; binds sword      [5.7.1.3]
    (Weapon.Bow damage = d) if (> d 10.0) => true             ; a guard          [5.7.1.2]
    (Weapon.Bow damage = _)               => false            ; _ binds nothing  [5.7.1.6]
    Weapon.Fist                           => false)           ; returns false    [5.7.1.1]
(match 3
    n if (> n 2) => "big"
    n            => "small")  ; returns "big"; each n is a separate binding  [5.7.1.3]
(match weapon
    (Weapon.Bow damage = d) => d
    _                       => "none")  ; error: an f64 arm and a string arm  [5.7.1.5]
(match weapon
    (Weapon.Bow damage = d) => true
    _                       => false)  ; warning: d is never used  [5.7.1.6]
(match weapon
    w if (== w Weapon.Fist) => "fist"
    _                       => "armed")  ; returns "fist"; a guard is any <Value bool>  [5.7.1.4]

;; 5.7.2 order and exhaustiveness
; 1. Arms are tried in order. The first arm that applies is chosen, and only
;    its value is evaluated.
; 2. A match must be exhaustive: for every possible value of type T, some
;    arm applies. A match that is not exhaustive is a compile error.
;    why?: a match always produces a U, so no value may be left without one
; 3. An arm with a guard does not count toward exhaustiveness.
;    why?: its guard may be false, and the compiler cannot know in general

(match 3
    n if (> n 1) => "more than one"
    3            => "three"
    _            => "other")  ; returns "more than one": the first arm applies first  [5.7.2.1]
(match 5
    1 => "one"
    2 => "two")  ; error: no arm for the other i32 values  [5.7.2.2]
(match true
    true  => "yes"
    false => "no")  ; exhaustive without _: bool has two values  [5.7.2.2]
(match weapon
    (Weapon.Sword sword = _)               => false
    (Weapon.Bow damage = d) if (> d 10.0)  => true
    Weapon.Fist                            => false)
    ; error: not exhaustive; the guarded arm does not cover every Bow  [5.7.2.3]

;; 5.7.3 next
; 1. An arm whose value is next shares the value of the arm after it. When
;    it applies, the match evaluates the next arm's value instead, without
;    checking that arm's pattern or guard. Chains of next arms share the
;    value at the end of the chain.
; 2. All arms that share one value must bind the same names, with the same
;    types.
; 3. The last arm cannot be next. That is a compile error.
;    why: the match would have no value for it, so it could not be
;    exhaustive

(let gunbow : Weapon = (Weapon.Bow damage = 5.0))
{match gunbow
    (Weapon.Sword sword = _) => {fn |-> string| "sword"}
    (Weapon.Bow damage = _)  => next
    Weapon.Fist              => {fn |-> string| "other"}}
    ; type: [-> string]; gunbow gets the Fist arm's value         [5.7.3.1]
    ; the Bow and Fist arms both bind nothing, so they may share  [5.7.3.2]
(match 2
    1 => next
    2 => next
    3 => "small"
    _ => "large")  ; returns "small": 2 shares the 3 arm's value  [5.7.3.1]
(match gunbow
    (Weapon.Bow damage = d) if (> d 10.0) => next
    (Weapon.Bow damage = d)               => (* d 2.0)
    _                                     => 0.0)
    ; returns 10.0; both shared arms bind d : f64  [5.7.3.2]
(match gunbow
    (Weapon.Bow damage = d) => next
    _                       => "other")
    ; error: the Bow arm binds d, and the arm it shares with binds nothing  [5.7.3.2]
(match 2
    1 => "one"
    _ => next)  ; error: the last arm cannot be next  [5.7.3.3]
(match gunbow
    _ => {fn |-> string| "any"})
    ; error: the arms are functions; write {match ...}  [2.4.2.3]

;;; 5.8 loop
(loop <Signature> <Value T>) -> T
; 1. Every parameter needs a default, which is its value on the first pass.
;    why?: a loop is never called, so nothing else could supply the values
; 2. The output type in the signature is the type of the whole loop, and is
;    required (2.5.2.1).
; 3. Each pass evaluates the body with the parameters bound. A recur in the
;    body starts the next pass (5.9).
; 4. The loop's value is the value of its first pass, where each recur
;    evaluates to the value of the pass it starts. When recur is only used
;    in tail position, that is the value of the last pass: the first one
;    that ends without calling recur.
; 5. When the output type is a function type, the loop is written in { },
;    and so are the forms inside that produce its value (2.4.2.1).

(loop |number : u32 = 1 -> Unit| (do
    (print (`hello ${number}` (to_string number)))
    (if (< number 3)
        (recur number = (+ number 1))
        (print "loop terminated!"))))  ; prints: hello 1  [5.8.3]
                                       ;         hello 2
                                       ;         hello 3
                                       ;         loop terminated!
(loop |n : u32 = 1 -> u32| (if (< n 9) (recur n = (* n 2)) n))
    ; returns 16, the value of the pass where n is 16  [5.8.4]
(loop |i : u32 = 0 total : u32 = 0 -> u32|
    (if (< i 4)
        (recur i = (+ i 1) total = (+ total i))
        total))  ; returns 6, which is 0 + 1 + 2 + 3  [5.8.3]
(loop |n : u32 -> u32| n)  ; error: n has no starting value      [5.8.1]
(loop |n : u32 = 0| n)     ; error: the output type is required  [5.8.2]
{loop |n : u32 = 0 -> [u32 -> u32]|
    {if (< n 3)
        {recur n = (+ n 1)}
        {make_adder n}}}  ; type: [u32 -> u32]; adds 3  [5.8.5]

;;; 5.9 recur
(recur <Binding>...) -> T
; Its bindings are written like a constructor's.
; 1. recur starts a new pass of the innermost loop or fn that contains it.
; 2. Each <Binding> gives a parameter its value for the new pass. Parameters
;    left out keep their current values.
; 3. A binding's name must be a parameter of that loop or fn.
; 4. recur evaluates to the value of the pass it starts, so its type T is
;    the output type of that loop or fn.
; 5. A recur is in tail position when its value becomes the value of the
;    whole pass: it is the body itself, or the last item of a do, a branch
;    of an if, the body of a when or unless, or an arm value of a match,
;    where that do, if, when, unless or match is itself in tail position.
; 6. recur may appear anywhere, but one that is not in tail position warns.
;    why: it helps learners avoid stack overflows
; 7. A recur in tail position runs in constant stack space: it is a jump,
;    not a call.

(loop |i : u32 = 0 acc : u32 = 1 -> u32|
    (if (< i 5)
        (recur i = (+ i 1) acc = (* acc 2))
        acc))  ; returns 32  [5.9.2]
(loop |i : u32 = 0 limit : u32 = 3 -> u32|
    (if (< i limit)
        (recur i = (+ i 1))  ; limit is left out, so it stays 3  [5.9.2]
        i))                  ; returns 3
(define factorial_2 = {fn |n : u64 acc : u64 = 1 -> u64|
    (if (== n 0)
        acc
        (recur n = (- n 1) acc = (* acc n)))})  ; recur in a fn needs no name  [5.9.1]
(factorial_2 5)  ; returns 120  [5.9.7]
(loop |i : u32 = 0 -> u32|
    (if (< i 2)
        (recur i = (+ i 1))          ; a new pass of the outer loop
        (loop |j : u32 = 10 -> u32|
            (if (< j 12)
                (recur j = (+ j 1))  ; a new pass of the inner loop  [5.9.1]
                (+ i j)))))          ; returns 14
(loop |n : u32 = 3 -> u32|
    (if (== n 0)
        0
        (+ n (recur n = (- n 1)))))
    ; warning: recur is not in tail position; returns 6  [5.9.4, 5.9.6]
(loop |i : u32 = 0 -> u32|
    (match (> i 2)
        true  => i
        false => (recur i = (+ i 1))))  ; returns 3; a match arm is a tail position  [5.9.5]
(loop |i : u32 = 0 -> u32| (if (< i 3) (recur j = 1) i))  ; error: j is not a parameter  [5.9.3]
(recur n = 1)  ; error: no loop or fn contains this recur                                [5.9.1]

;;; 5.10 for
(for |<Parameter>...| <Value bool> (recur <Binding>...) <Expression Unit>) -> Unit
; The step (recur ...) is written before the body, but runs after it.
; 1. for is sugar for a loop that returns Unit. Its parameters are written
;    with no ->, because the output is always Unit.
; 2. A for expands to:
(loop |<Parameter>... -> Unit|
    (if <Value bool>
        (do
            <Expression Unit>
            (recur <Binding>...))
        ()))
; 3. The condition is checked before every pass. If it holds, the body runs,
;    then the step starts the next pass. Otherwise the for returns ().
; 4. As in loop, every parameter needs a default (5.8.1).

(for |i : u32 = 0| (< i 3) (recur i = (+ i 1))
    (print (to_string i)))  ; prints: 0  [5.10.3]
                            ;         1
                            ;         2
(for |i : u32 = 10| (< i 3) (recur i = (+ i 1))
    (print "never"))  ; prints nothing: the condition fails on the first pass  [5.10.3]
(for |i : u32 = 0 j : u32 = 10| (< i j) (recur i = (+ i 1) j = (- j 1))
    (print (`${a}-${b}` (to_string i) (to_string j))))
    ; prints: 0-10, 1-9, 2-8, 3-7 and 4-6, one per line  [5.10.2]
(for |i : u32 = 0 -> Unit| (< i 3) (recur i = (+ i 1)) (print "x"))
    ; error: for takes no ->          [5.10.1]
(for |i : u32| (< i 3) (recur i = (+ i 1)) (print "x"))
    ; error: i has no starting value  [5.10.4]

;;;; 6 patterns
; A pattern either matches a value or does not. A pattern that matches may
; bind names, which are visible in its arm's guard and value (5.7.1.3).
; Examples use jane, the Employee from 4.4.2.

;;; 6.1 wildcard
_
; 1. _ matches any one value and binds nothing.

(match 42
    _ => "anything")  ; returns "anything"  [6.1.1]
(match (list 1 2)
    (list _ _) => "two items"
    _          => "other")  ; returns "two items"; each _ matches one element  [6.1.1]

;;; 6.2 identifiers
<Identifier>
; 1. An identifier that is not yet bound matches any value, and binds it to
;    that name.
; 2. An identifier that is already bound matches only a value equal to the
;    one it is bound to (8.3). This is how fieldless variants such as
;    Weapon.Fist, and constants such as PI, are matched.

(match 4
    n => (* n 2))  ; returns 8; n binds 4  [6.2.1]
(define LUCKY : i32 = 7)
(match 7
    LUCKY => "lucky"
    _     => "plain")  ; returns "lucky"; LUCKY is bound, so it is compared  [6.2.2]
(match Weapon.Fist
    Weapon.Fist => "fist"
    _           => "armed")  ; returns "fist"  [6.2.2]
(do
    (let target : i32 = 3)
    (match 5
        target => "hit"
        _      => "miss"))  ; returns "miss": target is bound, so it compares  [6.2.2]
(do
    (let target : i32 = 3)
    (match 5
        n => (+ n target)))  ; returns 8: n is not bound yet, so it binds  [6.2.1]

;;; 6.3 literals
<Literal>
; 1. A literal matches a value equal to it.

(match 'c'
    'a' => 1
    'c' => 3
    _   => 0)  ; returns 3  [6.3.1]
(match "hi"
    "hi" => true
    _    => false)  ; returns true  [6.3.1]
(match (Option.Some value = 0)
    (Option.Some value = 0) => "zero"
    (Option.Some value = _) => "other"
    Option.None             => "none")  ; returns "zero"; a literal inside a pattern  [6.3.1]

;;; 6.4 constructor patterns
(<Identifier> <Field Pattern>...)
(<Identifier> <Field Pattern>... ..)
; 1. A constructor pattern matches a struct or variant value built by that
;    constructor when each listed field's value matches its pattern.
; 2. The constructor's name is written in full, as when building:
;    (Option.Some value = v), not (Some value = v).
; 3. Each field is written name = pattern. (Option.Some value), with no
;    pattern, is an error.
; 4. Every field must be listed, unless the pattern ends with .., which
;    skips the remaining fields. .. may only come last.
; 5. A variant with no fields is matched by its name alone, without
;    parentheses (6.2.2).

(match (Option.Some value = 3)
    (Option.Some value = v) => v
    Option.None             => 0)  ; returns 3  [6.4.1]
(match (Option.Some value = (Option.Some value = 3))
    (Option.Some value = (Option.Some value = x)) => x
    (Option.Some value = Option.None)             => 0
    Option.None                                   => -1)
    ; returns 3; patterns nest  [6.4.1]
(match (Person.Hacker person = (Hacker skill = "backdoors" salary = 1.0))
    (Person.Hacker person = (Hacker skill = s ..))     => s
    (Person.Employee person = (Employee name = n ..))  => n)
    ; returns "backdoors"  [6.4.1]
(match (Option.Some value = 3)
    (Some value = v) => v
    _                => 0)  ; error: write Option.Some  [6.4.2]
(match (Option.Some value = 3)
    (Option.Some value) => 1
    _                   => 0)  ; error: the field value has no pattern  [6.4.3]
(match jane
    (Employee name = n age = _ dept = _ salary = _) => n)
    ; returns "jane"; every field is listed  [6.4.4]
(match jane
    (Employee name = n ..) => n)  ; returns "jane"; .. skips the other fields  [6.4.4]
(match jane
    (Employee dept = "unknown" ..) => "unassigned"
    _                              => "assigned")  ; returns "unassigned"  [6.4.1]
(match jane
    (Employee name = n) => n)  ; error: age, dept and salary are not listed  [6.4.4]
(match jane
    (Employee .. name = n) => n)  ; error: .. must come last  [6.4.4]
(do
    (let nothing : [Option i32] = Option.None)
    (match nothing
        Option.None             => "none"
        (Option.Some value = _) => "some"))  ; returns "none"  [6.4.5]

;;; 6.5 list patterns
(list <Pattern>...)
; 1. (list p...) matches a list whose elements match the patterns, in order.
;    Without .., the list must have exactly as many elements as there are
;    patterns.
; 2. .. matches zero or more elements and binds nothing.
; 3. .. may appear more than once, as long as no binding sits between two
;    of them.
;    why: otherwise which element the binding refers to is ambiguous

(let numbers : [List i32] = (list 1 2 3))
(match numbers
    (list 1 2 3)  => true    ; exactly 1, 2, 3                               [6.5.1]
    (list 1 2 ..) => true    ; starts with 1, 2                              [6.5.2]
    (list 1 .. 3) => false   ; starts with 1, ends with 3                    [6.5.2]
    _             => false)  ; returns true; _ is needed for exhaustiveness  [5.7.2.2]
(match (list 1 2 3 4 5)
    (list 1 .. 3 .. 5)     => true                 ; 1, any, 3, any, 5; 3 binds nothing    [6.5.3]
    (list a b .. e f)      => (> (+ a b) (+ e f))  ; binds the first two and the last two  [6.5.2]
    l @ (list 1 .. 3 .. 2) => (== l.2 5)           ; binds the whole list to l (6.6)
    _                      => false)               ; returns true, from the first arm      [5.7.2.1]
(match numbers
    (list)        => "empty"
    (list _)      => "one"
    (list _ _ ..) => "two or more")  ; returns "two or more"; exhaustive with no final _  [6.5.1]
(match numbers
    (list 1 3) => "one, three"
    _          => "other")  ; returns "other": without .., the length must match  [6.5.1]
(match numbers
    (list a .. b .. c) => b
    _                  => 0)
    ; error: b sits between two .., so the element it binds is ambiguous  [6.5.3]

;;; 6.6 whole-value binding
<Identifier> @ <Pattern>
; 1. name @ pattern matches when pattern does, and also binds the whole
;    value to name.

(match (Option.Some value = 4)
    whole @ (Option.Some value = v) if (> v 3) => whole
    _                                          => Option.None)
    ; returns (Option.Some value = 4)  [6.6.1]
(match (list 1 2 3)
    all @ (list 1 ..) => all.1
    _                 => 0)  ; returns 2; all is the whole list  [6.6.1]
(match jane
    e @ (Employee age = a ..) if (> a 18) => e.name
    _                                     => "minor")  ; returns "jane"  [6.6.1]

;;;; 7 generics

;;; 7.1 type parameters
; 1. Every type parameter must be declared: in a type header for a struct or
;    enum (7.2), or in a forall list for a function (7.3).
; There is no way yet to require that a type parameter supports an
; operation such as + (11.7.2).

(struct Box |item : T|)  ; error: T is not declared; write (struct [Box T] |item : T|)     [7.1.1]
{fn |x : T -> T| x}      ; error: T is not declared; write {fn [forall T] |x : T -> T| x}  [7.1.1]

;;; 7.2 generic structs and enums
(struct <Type Header> <Fields>) -> Unit
(enum <Type Header> <Definition>...) -> Unit
; 1. In the header [Name T U...], Name is the new generic type and T U...
;    are its type parameters. They can be used as types in every field.
; 2. A generic type is used by giving it type arguments: [Pair i32 string].
; 3. When a generic value is built, its type arguments are inferred from the
;    field values and the expected type.
;    why?: Option.None has no fields, so only the expected type can give it
;    its T
; 4. A generic struct's accessors are generic functions: Pair.first has type
;    [[forall A B] [Pair A B] -> A].

(struct [Pair A B] |
    first  : A
    second : B|)  ; A and B are used as field types  [7.2.1]
(let p : [Pair i32 string] = (Pair first = 1 second = "one"))  ; type arguments i32, string  [7.2.2]
(Pair first = 1 second = "one")                 ; type: [Pair i32 string], inferred          [7.2.3]
p.second                                        ; returns "one"                              [7.2.1]
(let q : [Pair i32] = p)                        ; error: Pair takes two type arguments       [7.2.2]
(let none_yet : [Option string] = Option.None)  ; T is string, from the expected type        [7.2.3]
(Pair.first p)                                  ; returns 1                                  [7.2.4]
Pair.first                                      ; type: [[forall A B] [Pair A B] -> A]       [7.2.4]
(enum [Either L R]
    Left |value : L|
    Right |value : R|)  ; a generic enum  [7.2.1]
(let e : [Either i32 string] = (Either.Right value = "r"))  ; L comes from the annotation  [7.2.3]
; hypothetical: assumes a type may refer to itself (11.3.8)
(enum [Tree T]
    Leaf
    Node |left : [Tree T] value : T right : [Tree T]|)
(let tree : [Tree i32] = (Tree.Node
    left = Tree.Leaf
    value = 5
    right = Tree.Leaf))

;;; 7.3 generic functions
{fn <Identifier>? <Forall> <Signature> <Value T>} -> [<Forall> <Type>... -> <Type>]
; 1. [forall T U...] declares the function's type parameters, which can be
;    used as types in its signature and body.
; 2. The function's type starts with the same forall list.
; 3. At each call, the type parameters are inferred from the arguments and
;    the expected type. Each parameter gets one type throughout the call.
; 4. Inside the body, a value whose type is a type parameter is not known to
;    be a function, so it is produced in ( ) (2.4.2.2).
;    why: T is not known to be a function there

(define first_or = {fn [forall T] |xs : [List T] fallback : T -> T|
    (match (get xs 0)  ; ( ) even though T could be a function  [7.3.4]
        (Option.Some value = first) => first
        Option.None                 => fallback)})
first_or                      ; type: [[forall T] [List T] T -> T]                        [7.3.2]
(first_or (list 4 5 6) 0)     ; returns 4; T is i32                                       [7.3.3]
(first_or (list) "none")      ; returns "none"; T is string                               [7.3.3]
(first_or (list 1 2) "none")  ; error: T cannot be both i32 and string                    [7.3.3]
{first_or (list {make_adder 1}) {make_adder 2}}
                              ; type: [u32 -> u32]; at this call T is a function, so { }  [2.4.2.2]
(define apply = {fn [forall T U] |x : T f : [T -> U] -> U| (f x)})
(apply 3 {fn |n : i32 -> bool| (> n 2)})  ; returns true; T is i32, U is bool             [7.3.3]
(apply jane Employee.name)    ; returns "jane"; an accessor is a function (4.4.3)         [7.3.1]
(define pair_up = {fn [forall A B] |a : A b : B -> [Pair A B]| (Pair first = a second = b)})
(pair_up 1 "one")             ; type: [Pair i32 string]                                   [7.3.1]

;;;; 8 built-ins

;;; 8.1 functions and special forms
; 1. A built-in that only takes input values and returns an output, with no
;    side effects, is a function. Like any function value, it can be bound,
;    passed and returned.
; 2. The other built-ins are special forms: print, panic, assert and debug.
;    Like keywords, they can only be called, never passed. A fn that calls
;    one is still an ordinary function value.
; 3. A built-in function that works on several types, such as +, has one
;    concrete type at each use. When called, it takes that type from its
;    arguments. When passed, it takes the type expected there.
; 4. In the same way, a passed list takes its number of parameters from the
;    expected function type (8.7.1).
; 5. The casts are generic functions (8.6). This file writes their type
;    argument first, as in (as i64 x). How type arguments are passed in
;    general is open (11.7.1).

(define add_u32 : [u32 u32 -> u32] = +)  ; + as a [u32 u32 -> u32]                     [8.1.3]
(define join : [string string -> string] = +)  ; + as string concatenation             [8.1.3]
(define plus = +)      ; error: no annotation, so + has no expected type               [8.1.3]
(define combine = {fn [forall T] |a : T b : T f : [T T -> T] -> T| (f a b)})
(combine 2 3 +)        ; returns 5; + is passed like any function                      [8.1.1]
(combine "ab" "cd" +)  ; returns "abcd"; here + is [string string -> string]           [8.1.3]
(combine 2 3 max)      ; returns 3                                                     [8.1.1]
(define three : [i32 i32 i32 -> [List i32]] = list)  ; list with three parameters      [8.1.4]
(three 1 2 3)          ; returns (list 1 2 3)                                          [8.1.4]
(define say : [string -> Unit] = print)  ; error: print is a special form              [8.1.2]
(define shout = {fn |s : string -> Unit| (print s)})  ; a fn that calls print is fine  [8.1.2]
(as i64 5)             ; the type argument i64 comes first                             [8.1.5]

;;; 8.2 arithmetic
(+ a b)        ; addition; numerical types and string
(- a b)        ; subtraction; numerical types
(* a b)        ; multiplication; numerical types
(/ a b)        ; division; numerical types
(% a b)        ; modulo, a mod b; numerical types
(pow a b)      ; a to the power b; numerical types
(abs a)        ; absolute value; numerical types
(min a b)      ; the smaller of a and b; numerical types
(max a b)      ; the larger of a and b; numerical types
(clamp a b c)  ; a, kept between b and c; numerical types
; 1. All operands have the same type, which is also the type of the result.
; 2. (- a) is (- 0 a): with one argument, the first parameter defaults to 0.
;    - is the only built-in whose default fills the first parameter.
;    why?: so (- 5) reads as negation
; 3. + on two strings joins them.
; 4. / on integers gives an integer, by rule 1. How it rounds negative
;    results, and what division by zero does, is open (11.5.5).
; 5. (clamp a b c) returns b if a < b, c if a > c, and a otherwise.

(+ 2 3)          ; returns 5                                            [8.2.1]
(+ 2 "cd")       ; error: an i32 and a string                           [8.2.1]
(let small : u8 = 1)
(+ small 2.0)    ; error: a u8 and an f64; nothing converts implicitly  [8.2.1]
(- 7 2)          ; returns 5                                            [8.2.1]
(- 5)            ; returns -5                                           [8.2.2]
(+ "ab" "cd")    ; returns "abcd"                                       [8.2.3]
(/ 7.0 2.0)      ; returns 3.5                                          [8.2.4]
(/ 7 2)          ; returns 3, an i32                                    [8.2.4]
(* 2.5 2.0)      ; returns 5.0                                          [8.2.1]
(% 7 3)          ; returns 1                                            [8.2.1]
(pow 2 10)       ; returns 1024                                         [8.2.1]
(abs -4)         ; returns 4                                            [8.2.1]
(min 3 8)        ; returns 3                                            [8.2.1]
(max 3 8)        ; returns 8                                            [8.2.1]
(clamp 15 0 10)  ; returns 10                                           [8.2.5]
(clamp -5 0 10)  ; returns 0                                            [8.2.5]
(clamp 5 0 10)   ; returns 5                                            [8.2.5]

;;; 8.3 comparison
(== a b)  ; equal
(< a b)   ; a < b
(> a b)   ; a > b
(<= a b)  ; a <= b
(>= a b)  ; a >= b
; 1. Both operands have the same type, and the result is a bool.
; 2. == works on every type except function types. Comparing functions is
;    a compile error.
;    why?: whether two functions are equal cannot be decided in general
; 3. NaN is not equal to NaN.
;    why?: this follows IEEE 754 floating point
; 4. <, >, <= and >= work on numerical types only.

(== 2 2)                            ; returns true                         [8.3.1]
(== 2 "2")                          ; error: an i32 and a string           [8.3.1]
(== "a" "b")                        ; returns false                        [8.3.2]
(== Weapon.Fist Weapon.Fist)        ; returns true                         [8.3.2]
(== {make_adder 1} {make_adder 1})  ; error: functions cannot be compared  [8.3.2]
; hypothetical: assumes (/ 0.0 0.0) is NaN (11.5.5)
(let nan : f64 = (/ 0.0 0.0))
(== nan nan)  ; returns false                      [8.3.3]
(< 2 3)       ; returns true                       [8.3.4]
(>= 3 3)      ; returns true                       [8.3.4]
(<= 4 3)      ; returns false                      [8.3.4]
(> 2.5 2.4)   ; returns true                       [8.3.4]
(< "a" "b")   ; error: < needs numerical operands  [8.3.4]

;;; 8.4 boolean logic
(not a)    ; logical not
(and a b)  ; logical and
(or a b)   ; logical or
(xor a b)  ; logical exclusive or
; 1. The operands and the result are bools.
; 2. and, or and xor are eager. As functions (8.1.1), they evaluate both
;    operands before the call, even when the first one decides the result.

(not true)                    ; returns false                                              [8.4.1]
(and true false)              ; returns false                                              [8.4.1]
(or true false)               ; returns true                                               [8.4.1]
(xor true true)               ; returns false                                              [8.4.1]
(and 1 0)                     ; error: the operands must be bools                          [8.4.1]
(or true (do (print "evaluated") false))  ; prints: evaluated; returns true                [8.4.2]
(let numbers : [List i32] = (list 1 2 3))
(and false (== numbers.9 1))  ; panics: index out of range; the second operand still runs  [8.4.2]

;;; 8.5 bitwise
(bit_not a)    ; bitwise not
(bit_and a b)  ; bitwise and
(bit_or a b)   ; bitwise or
(bit_xor a b)  ; bitwise exclusive or
(shl a n)      ; shift a left by n bits
(shr a n)      ; shift a right by n bits
; 1. These work on all stack types, which include the integer types. What
;    else counts as a stack type is open (11.3.2).
; 2. n defaults to 1 in shl and shr.

(let b : u8 = 12)  ; binary 00001100               [8.5.1]
(bit_and b 10)     ; returns 8, binary 00001000    [8.5.1]
(bit_or b 10)      ; returns 14, binary 00001110   [8.5.1]
(bit_xor b 10)     ; returns 6, binary 00000110    [8.5.1]
(bit_not b)        ; returns 243, binary 11110011  [8.5.1]
(shl b 2)          ; returns 48                    [8.5.1]
(shr b 2)          ; returns 3                     [8.5.1]
(shl b)            ; returns 24                    [8.5.2]

;;; 8.6 casting and conversion
(the <Type> a)     ; ascription: a, typed as <Type>
(as <Type> a)      ; widening
(try_as <Type> a)  ; checked: returns [Option <Type>]
(wrap <Type> a)    ; wrapping: wraps around on overflow
(parse <Type> a)   ; parsing a string: returns [Result <Type> ParseError]
(to_string a)      ; text: works on every type
; 1. The casts are the only way to change a value's type (3.1.3).
; 2. the gives a value its type at compile time. It is how a literal is
;    written with a type other than its default (5.1.1.3), and it is native
;    for numerical types.
; 3. as only compiles when the target type is wider than the value's type,
;    so it never fails.
; 4. try_as returns Option.None when the value does not fit the target type.
; 5. wrap wraps around on overflow. It is native for numerical types.
; 6. parse reads a string as a value of the target type. It returns
;    Result.Error when the string is not a valid value of that type.
; 7. to_string turns a value of any type into text.

(let n : u32 = 7)
(let m : u64 = n)        ; error: nothing converts implicitly; write (as u64 n)    [8.6.1]
(the u8 5)               ; type: u8                                                [8.6.2]
(as i64 5)               ; returns 5 as an i64; i64 is wider than i32              [8.6.3]
(as u8 5)                ; error: u8 is not wider than i32                         [8.6.3]
(try_as u8 200)          ; returns (Option.Some value = 200), holding a u8         [8.6.4]
(try_as u8 300)          ; returns Option.None: 300 does not fit in a u8           [8.6.4]
(wrap u8 300)            ; returns 44, which is 300 - 256                          [8.6.5]
(parse u32 "42")         ; returns (Result.Ok value = 42)                          [8.6.6]
(parse u32 "forty-two")  ; returns a Result.Error holding a ParseError             [8.6.6]
(to_string 42)           ; returns "42"                                            [8.6.7]
(to_string excalibur)    ; returns excalibur as text; the format is open (11.7.6)  [8.6.7]

;;; 8.7 lists

;; 8.7.1 list
(list <Value T>...) -> [List T]
; 1. list returns a list of its arguments, in order.
; 2. Called directly, list takes any number of arguments. Passed as a value,
;    it takes the parameter count of the expected type (8.1.4).
; 3. (list) is an empty list. Its T comes from the expected type.

(list 1 2 3)                      ; type: [List i32]                           [8.7.1.1]
(list "a" "b")                    ; type: [List string]                        [8.7.1.1]
(list (list 1) (list 2 3))        ; type: [List [List i32]]; two arguments     [8.7.1.2]
(define pair_of : [u32 u32 -> [List u32]] = list)  ; list with two parameters  [8.7.1.2]
(let empty : [List u8] = (list))  ; T is u8, from the annotation               [8.7.1.3]

;; 8.7.2 get
(get <Value [List T]> <Value u32>) -> [Option T]
; 1. get returns (Option.Some value = x) when x is the element at the
;    index, and Option.None when the index is out of range.
; 2. The index can be any u32 value, not just a literal (compare 3.4.2).

(let numbers : [List i32] = (list 1 2 3))
(get numbers 1)        ; returns (Option.Some value = 2)         [8.7.2.1]
(get numbers 5)        ; returns Option.None: 5 is out of range  [8.7.2.1]
(get numbers (+ 1 1))  ; returns (Option.Some value = 3)         [8.7.2.2]
; hypothetical: len is not defined yet (11.7.7)
(len numbers)  ; returns 3

;;; 8.8 output and failure
(print <Value string>) -> Unit
(panic <Value string>?) -> Never
(assert <Value bool> <Value string>?) -> Unit
(debug <Value>) -> Unit
; All four are special forms (8.1.2).
; 1. print writes its string to the console, then a newline. It only takes
;    a string; convert other values with to_string (8.6).
; 2. panic stops the program with its message, which defaults to "panic". It
;    never returns.
; 3. assert does nothing when its condition is true. When it is false, it
;    panics with its message, which defaults to "assertion failed".
; 4. debug writes a value of any type to the console. The compiler handles
;    it specially.
; 5. debug is an anti-pattern outside of debugging.
;    why: it can result in nonsense being printed

(print "hello")                 ; prints: hello                         [8.8.1]
(print 42)                      ; error: print takes a string           [8.8.1]
(print (to_string 42))          ; prints: 42                            [8.8.1]
(panic)                         ; panics: panic                         [8.8.2]
(panic "out of fuel")           ; panics: out of fuel                   [8.8.2]
(assert (== 1 1))               ; returns ()                            [8.8.3]
(assert (== 1 2))               ; panics: assertion failed              [8.8.3]
(assert (== 1 2) "math broke")  ; panics: math broke                    [8.8.3]
(debug excalibur)               ; prints excalibur in a debugging form  [8.8.4]
(debug (list 1 2))              ; any type works                        [8.8.4]
; as a whole program:
(define main = {fn |-> Unit| (debug "left in by mistake")})
    ; compiles, but debug is not meant to stay in a program  [8.8.5]

;;;; 9 programs

;;; 9.1 main
(define main = {fn |-> Unit| <Value Unit>})
; 1. Every program defines main, a function of type [-> Unit]. Running the
;    program calls main.
; Most examples in this file leave main out (0.3.1). Exit codes are open
; (11.8.3).

(define main = {fn |-> Unit| ()})  ; the smallest program  [9.1.1]
; a different program:
(define main = {fn |-> Unit| (print "hello world!")})  ; prints: hello world!  [9.1.1]

;;; 9.2 memory
; 1. Memory is managed by reference counting, which programs never see.
;    why: memory management is not a priority
; 2. A value stays alive while anything refers to it. A function value
;    keeps alive the values its body uses (5.4.1.4), even after the block
;    that bound them has ended.

(define main = {fn |-> Unit| (do
    (do
        (let big : [List i32] = (list 1 2 3))
        ())                                ; big is freed here, with no code to free it   [9.2.1]
    (let add_ten : [u32 -> u32] = {do
        (let ten : u32 = 10)
        {fn |y : u32 -> u32| (+ y ten)}})  ; the fn keeps ten alive after its block ends  [9.2.2]
    (print (to_string (add_ten 5))))})     ; prints: 15                                   [9.2.2]

;;; 9.3 mod (todo)
; mod imports a module, and :: reaches into one. Neither is designed yet
; (11.8.1).

;;; 9.4 export (todo)
; export makes a define visible to other modules, and only defines can be
; exported. It is not designed yet (11.8.1).

;;; 9.5 sugar
; 1. All syntactic sugar, such as for (5.10) and fancy strings (5.1.2), is
;    built in. Programs cannot define their own yet; macros are planned
;    (11.8.5).

(for |i : u32 = 0| (< i 1) (recur i = (+ i 1)) (print "once"))
    ; built-in sugar for a loop (5.10.2); prints: once  [9.5.1]

;;;; 10 example programs
; Each entry is one complete program. Tags point at the rules each part
; relies on.

;;; 10.1 fizzbuzz
(define fizzbuzz = {fn |n : u32 -> string|
    (match n
        k if (== (% k 15) 0) => "FizzBuzz"        ; k is not bound yet, so it binds n      [6.2.1]
        k if (== (% k 3) 0)  => "Fizz"            ; arms are tried in order                [5.7.2.1]
        k if (== (% k 5) 0)  => "Buzz"
        k                    => (to_string k))})  ; the unguarded arm makes it exhaustive  [5.7.2.3]
(define main = {fn |-> Unit|
    (for |i : u32 = 1| (<= i 15) (recur i = (+ i 1))
        (print (fizzbuzz i)))})  ; fizzbuzz is defined above, but could be below  [4.1.2]
                                 ; prints: 1, 2, Fizz, 4, Buzz, Fizz, 7, 8, Fizz, Buzz,
                                 ;         11, Fizz, 13, 14, FizzBuzz, one per line

;;; 10.2 summing a list
(define sum = {fn |xs : [List i32] -> i32|
    (loop |i : u32 = 0 total : i32 = 0 -> i32|
        (match (get xs i)
            (Option.Some value = x) => (recur
                i = (+ i 1)
                total = (+ total x))              ; in tail position, so a jump  [5.9.7]
            Option.None             => total))})  ; past the end of xs           [8.7.2.1]
(define main = {fn |-> Unit| (do
    (print (to_string (sum (list 3 4 5))))  ; prints: 12
    (print (to_string (sum (list)))))})     ; prints: 0; (list) is a [List i32] here  [8.7.1.3]

;;; 10.3 shapes
(struct Point |x : i32 y : i32|)
(enum Shape
    Square |side : i32 corner : Point|
    Rect   |width : i32 height : i32 corner : Point|
    Dot    |at : Point|)
(define area = {fn |shape : Shape -> i32|
    (match shape
        (Shape.Square side = s ..)           => (* s s)  ; .. skips corner               [6.4.4]
        (Shape.Rect width = w height = h ..) => (* w h)
        (Shape.Dot at = _)                   => 0)})  ; one arm per variant: exhaustive  [5.7.2.2]
(define name_of = {fn |shape : Shape -> string|
    (match shape
        (Shape.Square ..) => "square"
        (Shape.Rect ..)   => "rect"
        (Shape.Dot ..)    => "dot")})
(define describe = {fn |shape : Shape -> string|
    (`${kind} with area ${size}`
        (name_of shape)
        (to_string (area shape)))})  ; kind first, then size  [5.1.2.1]
(define main = {fn |-> Unit| (do
    (let origin : Point = (Point x = 0 y = 0))
    (let corner : Point = (Point ..origin x = 5))  ; y is copied from origin  [4.4.4.2]
    (let rect : Shape = (Shape.Rect width = 2 height = 3 corner = corner))
    (print (describe rect))                        ; prints: rect with area 6
    (print (describe (Shape.Square side = 4 corner = origin)))
                                                   ; prints: square with area 16
    (print (describe (Shape.Dot at = origin))))})  ; prints: dot with area 0

;;; 10.4 parsing with Result
(define parse_age = {fn |text : string -> [Result u32 string]|
    (match (parse u32 text)
        (Result.Ok value = n) if (> n 150) => (Result.Error error = "too old")
        (Result.Ok value = n)              => (Result.Ok value = n)
        (Result.Error error = _)           =>
            (Result.Error error = (`not a number: ${t}` text)))})
; The second arm rebuilds the Ok. Returning the matched value itself would
; not compile: it is a [Result u32 ParseError], not a [Result u32 string].
(define report = {fn |text : string -> string|
    (match (parse_age text)
        (Result.Ok value = n)    => (`age ${n}` (to_string n))
        (Result.Error error = e) => (`error: ${e}` e))})
(define main = {fn |-> Unit| (do
    (print (report "42"))      ; prints: age 42
    (print (report "200"))     ; prints: error: too old
    (print (report "abc")))})  ; prints: error: not a number: abc

;;; 10.5 functions as values
(define make_adder = {fn |x : u32 -> [u32 -> u32]| {fn |y : u32 -> u32| (+ x y)}})
(define compose = {fn [forall A B C] |f : [A -> B] g : [B -> C] -> [A -> C]|
    {fn |x : A -> C| (g (f x))}})  ; ( ) twice: B and C are type parameters  [7.3.4]
(define fold = {fn [forall T U] |xs : [List T] init : U step : [U T -> U] -> U|
    (loop |i : u32 = 0 acc : U = init -> U|
        (match (get xs i)
            (Option.Some value = x) => (recur i = (+ i 1) acc = (step acc x))
            Option.None             => acc))})
(define main = {fn |-> Unit| (do
    (let numbers : [List i32] = (list 1 2 3 4))
    (print (to_string (fold numbers 0 +)))      ; + as [i32 i32 -> i32]; prints: 10       [8.1.3]
    (print (to_string (fold numbers 1 *)))      ; prints: 24
    (print (to_string (fold numbers 0 max)))    ; prints: 4
    (print (fold (list "a" "b" "c") "" +))      ; + as string concatenation; prints: abc  [8.1.3]
    (let inc_then_double : [u32 -> u32] = {compose
        {make_adder 1}
        {fn |n : u32 -> u32| (* n 2)}})         ; annotated: not a literal                [4.3.2]
    (print (to_string (inc_then_double 5))))})  ; prints: 12                              [5.4.1.4]

;;;; 11 open questions
; Each item is something this file does not settle yet. (old todo) marks an
; item from the previous todo list.

;;; 11.1 lexical
; 1. Which characters may an identifier contain? Is x-1 one identifier, or x
;    followed by -1? Can qualified names chain, as in a.b.c?
; 2. Which number formats exist besides 12 and 3.14: 0xFF, 0b1010, 1_000,
;    1e10, 5., .5?
; 3. Is a char a byte or a Unicode scalar value? What encoding do strings
;    use?
; 4. What does a backslash before any other character mean? Are \r, \0 or
;    \u{...} needed?
; 5. Is whitespace optional around other punctuation: ->, =, =>, ., .., @?
; 6. What does a doc comment (;:) attach to, and what reads it?

;;; 11.2 grammar
; 1. May a program bind a name used by a built-in special form, such as
;    print?
; 2. Which keywords are restricted to certain places? define is top level
;    only (4.2.1). May struct and enum appear inside a do? (old todo: "remove
;    status of certain keywords as Values so they can only be called in
;    certain contexts"; 2.2.1 now settles the Values part.)

;;; 11.3 types
; 1. 128-bit (quad) and arbitrary-size (big) numbers. (old notes: "will be
;    implemented later")
; 2. Which types are "stack types" for the bitwise operations? Do floats,
;    bool and char count?
; 3. Is char a numerical type? Can chars be compared with <?
; 4. Does Never fit wherever another type is expected? Is (if c 5 (panic))
;    legal?
; 5. What is ParseError?
; 6. How is a [Map K V] built, read and matched? (todo)
; 7. Type aliases. (old todo)
; 8. May a type refer to itself, as [Tree T] does in 7.2?
; 9. Is a struct with no fields allowed, such as (struct Empty)? Is its value
;    written Empty or (Empty)?

;;; 11.4 declarations
; 1. May let bind a name that is already bound (shadowing), in the same
;    block or in an inner one?
; 2. What happens with a cycle among defines, such as (define A : u32 = B)
;    with (define B : u32 = A)?
; 3. May a define's value have side effects, such as a print? When do they
;    run?
; 4. In (Employee ..source ...), must ..source come first? May it appear
;    more than once? May the source be any value, or only an identifier?
; 5. Can a constructor such as Employee or Option.Some be passed as a
;    function value, given that it takes its arguments by name?

;;; 11.5 expressions
; 1. In what order are a call's arguments evaluated?
; 2. Can an integer literal take a float type, as in (let x : f64 = 5)? If
;    so, (let r : f64 = (if true 5 2.5)) would compile while
;    (if true 5 2.5) does not (5.5.3).
; 3. Is a literal that does not fit its type, such as (the u8 300), a
;    compile error?
; 4. What happens on overflow in +, - and *? wrap exists for wraparound on
;    purpose.
; 5. Does integer / round toward zero or down? What happens on division by
;    zero, for integers and for floats?
; 6. What sign does % give for negative operands?
; 7. What does integer pow do with a negative exponent?
; 8. What does (clamp a b c) return when b > c?
; 9. May a call bind arguments by name, as in (f x = 1), the way recur and
;    constructors do?
; 10. When is a default value evaluated: once, or at each call? May it
;     refer to earlier parameters?
; 11. Does an arm that can never apply, such as one after a _ arm, warn?
; 12. Literal handling and arithmetic in general. (old todo)

;;; 11.6 patterns
; 1. May the fields of a constructor pattern appear in any order?
; 2. A bound identifier in a pattern compares by value (6.2.2). What if it
;    is bound to a function, which == cannot compare (8.3.2)?
; 3. May a bound identifier, which compares rather than binds, sit between
;    two .. in a list pattern (6.5.3)?

;;; 11.7 generics and built-ins
; 1. How does a call pass a type argument, as the casts do (8.6)? Employee
;    is both a type and a constructor, so a parser cannot always tell a type
;    argument from a value argument. (todo)
; 2. How can a generic function require an operation on a type parameter,
;    such as +? (old todo: traits for generics)
; 3. What does (list) mean when no type is expected?
; 4. Is == structural on structs, enums and lists? What about a struct with
;    a function field?
; 5. Which conversions count as wider for as? Is i32 to f64 wider? i64 to
;    f64? u32 to i64? What does try_as do with a float that has a fraction,
;    and how are floats turned into integers?
; 6. What text does to_string produce for floats, bools, structs, enums and
;    lists? What does debug print?
; 7. Which list operations exist: len, push, map, filter, fold? (len is used
;    hypothetically in 8.7.2.) Does get work on strings or maps?
; 8. Can the operand of the be any value, or only a literal?

;;; 11.8 programs
; 1. Modules: mod, export and ::. (old todo)
; 2. Input and output beyond print, such as reading input or files. (old
;    todo)
; 3. Command-line arguments, and exit codes from main.
; 4. More error management, such as passing a Result.Error along without a
;    match at every step. (old todo)
; 5. Macros, so programs can define their own sugar. (old notes: "macros
;    will come later")

;;; 11.9 hypothetical examples
; Examples marked hypothetical:, and what each one assumes.
; 1. 7.2: [Tree T] refers to itself (11.3.8).
; 2. 8.3: (/ 0.0 0.0) produces NaN (11.5.5).
; 3. 8.7.2: len exists (11.7.7).
